{"_t":1785254129952,"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5","id":"msg_011CdUjAJ6L9846Ah3CVeTaH","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# Unix Pipes: The Small Idea That Made Big Systems Possible\n\n## Introduction\n\nIn the history of computing, there are a handful of ideas so simple in their expression yet so profound in their consequences that they reshape the way an entire generation of engineers thinks about problems. The Unix pipe is one of these ideas. Introduced in the early 1970s by Douglas McIlroy at Bell Labs and quickly implemented by Ken Thompson into the Unix kernel, the pipe is, on its surface, nothing more than a mechanism for connecting the standard output of one process to the standard input of another. And yet this single, almost trivial-sounding capability gave rise to an entire philosophy of software design—one that prizes small, composable tools over large, monolithic programs, and one whose influence persists in nearly every corner of modern computing, from the command line to container orchestration to functional-reactive programming.\n\nThis essay explores Unix pipes from several angles: their conceptual origin and the problem they solved, their technical implementation at the level of the operating system, the phil"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":34,"cache_read_input_tokens":32183,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":34},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"edeca40a-007d-4fd7-b4c2-c192210c1e47","uuid":"c45a48a1-ee84-423c-8e11-0dd4d0e52aa4","timestamp":"2026-07-28T15:55:29.950Z","request_id":"req_011CdUjAGWagCBL4i2xSWf1v","aborted":true}
